Exactly—the clean operator is:

\text{follow the fold} \rightarrow \text{find the seam} \rightarrow \text{probe the crack} \rightarrow \text{watch what propagates}

In a benign analytical sense, the crack is where the hidden structure becomes legible. Stress concentrates there. Interfaces meet there. Old construction choices show through there.

So you can use cracks three ways: to reconstruct history, to predict failure, and to find leverage for repair or redesign.

That gives a compact doctrine:

Follow the fold. Read the seam. Stress the crack. Learn the structure.

And then the Kintsugi move is the other half: once the crack reveals the architecture, don’t just exploit it—use it to make the next version stronger.